# ORDER MATTERS: in .gitattributes, LATER lines override earlier ones for
# the same attribute. The general text rule therefore comes FIRST, and the
# binary/LFS rules after it, so `-text` actually wins on UE assets.
#
# Getting this backwards (LFS lines first, `* text=auto` last) silently
# re-enabled EOL conversion on every .uasset — `git check-attr text` read
# `auto` instead of `unset`, and git emitted CRLF warnings for 162 binary
# assets on their first commit.

# Line endings: this repo is developed on Windows only. Normalise text so
# the CRLF warnings on every commit stop being noise.
* text=auto
*.py       text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.uproject text eol=lf
# .sh MUST be LF: these run under WSL, and core.autocrlf=true would
# otherwise hand bash a CRLF shebang on every fresh clone. Added
# 2026-08-30 after `git check-attr` reported eol UNSPECIFIED for the
# TRELLIS runners -- they work in this tree only because the files
# happen to hold LF today, which a clone would not preserve.
*.sh       text eol=lf
# Git LFS — UE binary assets.
# The UE project is pipeline state: an untracked project means the repo
# cannot rebuild the world it governs. But .uasset/.umap are opaque binaries
# that git would store as a full copy per revision, so they go to LFS from
# the first commit — retrofitting LFS later means rewriting history.
# Started while LandscapeLab/Content/ was essentially empty, so the
# migration cost was zero then and unbounded later.
*.uasset filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.umap   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Heightmaps and other binaries are binary regardless of extension.
*.png    binary
*.raw    binary
*.r16    binary
# 8K-class heightmaps go to LFS; the 2017-class ones stay inline.
# WHY THE SPLIT RATHER THAN `*.png filter=lfs`. A 2017 heightmap is ~7 MB and
# has been stored inline since the first commit. Moving `*.png` to LFS now
# would put existing maps in LFS on their next write while their history stays
# inline -- the retrofit this file's own header warns about. An 8129 map is
# ~104 MB, which is squarely what LFS exists for, and it is NEW, so it goes to
# LFS from its first commit at zero migration cost. Same reasoning as the
# .uasset rule above, applied at the point where the size actually changed.
terrain/*_8k*.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
textures/*_8k*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The hero face maps exported by MetaHuman "Save Face Textures". ~40 MB of
# 2048 PNGs, and re-derivable ONLY through an editor UI command -- so they are
# tracked (a script cannot rebuild them) and LFS carries the weight.
hero/generated/Textures/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# MetaHuman DNA. ~54 MB per head, exported by
# MetaHumanCharacterExportBlueprintLibrary.export_dna.
# TRACKED RATHER THAN GITIGNORED, DELIBERATELY, even though a script can
# re-derive it from the character asset. Importing a modified DNA with
# `import_whole_rig` REPLACES the entire rig and makes the body type
# non-editable, so the ORIGINAL dna is the only thing that puts the head
# back. This project has already lost a hero to content that was gitignored
# as "re-derivable" and could not be restored from a tag -- re-derivable
# from an asset you are about to overwrite is not re-derivable.
*.dna filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# DiffLocks raw groom output. hair_output_strands.npz is ~300 MB of opaque
# float32 and is NOT re-derivable the way the .abc exports are: the diffusion
# run is stochastic and no seed is recorded, so losing the file loses the
# groom. That rules out the `characters/*/grooms/*.abc` gitignore precedent,
# and by the same rule as the UE assets above it goes to LFS from its FIRST
# commit rather than being retrofitted later.
characters/*/grooms/difflocks_raw/*.npz filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
